    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_86c8ae8aa87684380fd725c77d83e2274d75b947861e8a80f36b25b0df60953c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The span supports three deliberately separate claims. Under RH, the displayed Ratios-side residual has main contribution −g(0)/2 and a remainder whose exponent deteriorates with σ; for σ<1/3 that remainder is absorbable into square-root-scale error. Independently of the Ratios Conjecture, Theorem 1.2 proves family-specific comparisons with its prediction: error O(X^{−(1−σ)/2+ε}) for even fundamental discriminants, and a three-term error for the 8d family that is O(X^{−1/2+ε}) when σ<1/3. Finally, one lower-order term contains ζ′/ζ near 1 and is therefore controlled by nontrivial zeros of ζ. Thus restricted support buys stronger remainder control in these estimates, while the logarithmic derivative supplies a concrete arithmetic channel for secondary structure; neither the comparison nor the lower-order mechanism proves the Ratios Conjecture in general.",
