    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_cb7ed54423283f34a635e5e97787921444e22fcf77af8ef71bedc2a51615eb06",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Three auxiliary inputs assemble the source's spectral-to-arithmetic comparison. The explicit formula turns zeros into conductor/Gamma terms and a full prime-power sum. Averaging that identity over the family then needs local discriminant frequencies: Möbius and congruence counting determine the proportion with a given prime divisor, which supplies the even-prime-power correction. The same cumulative family count, transformed by partial summation, evaluates the oscillatory conductor phase needed when the Ratios-side expression is moved off the real line. Thus contour representation, local Euler data, and a family-scale transform provide complementary inputs to the comparison rather than independent appendices.",
