    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f59714a66e66af96656e8031393fe6b801dea2ae04a6bdc023d66f24b0e0aa9a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source escalates methods at a precisely identified bottleneck. Jutila's mean-square estimate and Cauchy-Schwarz give a simple support-dependent power saving for the odd prime-power contribution, but not the desired square-root scale. The stronger route smooths the family boundary, splits squarefreeness, applies Poisson summation, separates zero, square, and nonsquare dual frequencies, repairs the nonsquare estimate by using different transform-derivative bounds below and above a large dual cutoff, balances the smoothing and squarefree parameters against the support-induced prime cutoff, and finally transfers the interval estimate to the initial-segment family dyadically.",
